{"type": "result", "key": "v2:d33e28521d4e4b209d6258556570cbfcc773b42d6786442ceb4a8a6d86dfd838", "agentId": "aac477eaef72f73d0", "result": {"experiment": "E6 - is round 1's extra cost Claude Code's harness, and would pi (OMP / pi-ai) change the conclusion", "completed": "Partly. No model call was made through pi; the pi side is a paper comparison built from captured request shapes and measured token counts.\n\nDone:\n- **Pi facts, no model calls:** install, SDK source, credential type, and what the caller controls.\n- **Request capture at a local sink:** Claude Code 2.1.296 (4 requests) and pi-ai 18.9.2 (11 variants), same prompt, tools, view and message. Pi was driven with dummy tokens, so the owner's OMP credential was never loaded.\n- **Live through Claude Code (43 Haiku, 9 Sonnet):** token size of the two blocks Claude Code adds, one stock-configuration call, round 1's E2 turn sequence and mid-view-change sequence replayed with 5-minute marks, and a 5-minute expiry check.\n- **Repricing:** round 1's E2 and E3 rows recomputed for the pi request shape.\n\nNot done:\n- **Live pi replay (0 of 400 calls).** OMP's Anthropic credential is the subscription login, so the stated rule allowed it. I still did not run it, for two reasons:\n  - With that credential pi-ai cannot send the gist's request. It prepends a Claude Code billing header with a computed attestation hash and the line \"You are Claude Code, Anthropic's official CLI for Claude.\", and sends `User-Agent: claude-cli/2.1.280` and Claude Code beta flags. The route works by presenting itself to Anthropic as Claude Code.\n  - That puts the owner's account at risk in a way his message did not ask for, and the go-ahead came from the workflow script. It needs his explicit yes.\n- **The exact gist shape on pi.** pi-ai produces it only with an API key, which is pay-per-token and excluded.\n- **A 30-turn E3 slice.** Round 1's chats have 26 turns at the gist's limits and 42 scaled; I repriced those.\n- **Sonnet confirmation** is 8 warm turns, not a full sequence.\n- **Local llama.cpp routers** were not used. Listeners were up on the tailnet address (ports 84xx; /v1/models not probed), but a local model says nothing about Anthropic cache pricing.", "calls_made": "Round-2 ledger (`ledger-r2.jsonl`), E6 rows only: Haiku 43 of 300, Sonnet 9 of 60, Opus 0, pi 0 of 400.\n\n| Phase | Haiku | Sonnet |\n|---|---|---|\n| thin (block sizes) | 5 | 0 |\n| stock (owner's normal config) | 1 | 0 |\n| turns5m | 25 | 9 |\n| mid5m | 9 | 0 |\n| gap5m | 3 | 0 |\n\n- No rate-limit or usage-limit error occurred.\n- Sink captures reached no model: 3 Claude Code runs and 11 pi-ai requests went to 127.0.0.1 and got HTTP 400. Their reservations are in `capture/sink-ledger.jsonl`, not the shared ledger.", "results_table": "**1. Fixed overhead per call** (input-side tokens, message \"Reply with exactly: OK\", no view)\n\n| Request | Tokens | Basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stock Claude Code, owner's normal config | 27,295 | measured, n=1 |\n| Thinned Claude Code (round-1 flags) | 2,253 | measured, n=2 |\n| - tools + system entry | 1,641 | measured |\n| - reminder block before the view (559 bytes) | 170 | measured by difference, n=1 |\n| - environment block after the message (946 bytes) | 357 | measured by difference, n=1 |\n| pi-ai, subscription shape | about 1,726 | inferred (2,253 - 170 - 357) |\n\nRequest body for the same content: Claude Code 11,821 bytes, pi subscription shape 9,976, pi API-key shape 9,585.\n\n**2. Request shape**\n\n| | Claude Code 2.1.296 | pi-ai, subscription login | pi-ai, API key |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Before the gist prompt | billing header, Agent SDK identity line | billing header with per-request hash, \"You are Claude Code\" line | nothing |\n| Tool names | `mcp__chat__zoom`, `mcp__chat__date` | `_zoom`, `_date` | `zoom`, `date` |\n| Before the view | reminder block with login email | nothing | nothing |\n| After the message | environment block (cwd, OS, model, date) | nothing | nothing |\n| Cache marks | all 4 used: identity, system, our view block, environment tail | 3 by default (last tool, system, last message block); all 4 caller-owned via `onPayload` | same as subscription |\n| Default lifetime | 1 hour; 5 minutes via `CLAUDE_CODE_PROMPT_CACHE_TTL=5m` | 1 hour; 5 minutes via `cacheRetention: \"short\"` | 5 minutes; 1 hour via `\"long\"` |\n| Changes between calls | nothing before the view | 5 hex characters of the billing hash in system[0] | nothing |\n\n**3. E2 turn sequence, same bytes as round 1** (input-side cost per warm turn = input + W x write + 0.1 x read)\n\n| Arm | Haiku, n=24 | Read share | Sonnet, n=8 | Read share |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Claude Code, 1-hour, W=2 (round 1, measured) | 8,090 | 96.4% | 7,386 | 95.4% |\n| Claude Code, 5-minute, W=1.25 (measured this round) | 6,799 | 96.4% | 6,030 | 95.4% |\n| pi, 1-hour (paper) | 7,359 | 97.1% | 6,655 | 96.3% |\n| pi, 5-minute (paper) | 6,336 | 97.1% | 5,567 | 96.3% |\n\n- Token counts were identical to round 1 on 24 of 24 Haiku turns and 8 of 8 Sonnet turns; only the write class changed (Haiku: 41,292 tokens all 5-minute against all 1-hour in round 1).\n- The gist's figure is 98.6%.\n- Mid-view change with 5-minute marks (Haiku, 9 calls) matched round 1 exactly: a middle-line change rewrote 27,034 tokens; a change 10 lines from the end after growth rewrote 2,401.\n- 5-minute expiry (n=1, 15.9 KB view): read in full after 20 s; after a 330 s pause all 7,272 tokens were written again.\n\n**4. E3 chats, turn calls only** (cost = input + W x write + 0.1 x read + 5 x output)\n\n| Arm | Sonnet, 26 turns | Haiku, 26 turns | Haiku scaled, 42 turns |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| UniiChat on Claude Code, 1-hour (round 1, measured) | 110,260 | 124,750 | 219,094 |\n| UniiChat on Claude Code, 5-minute (repriced) | 88,560 | 106,989 | 183,930 |\n| UniiChat on pi, 1-hour (paper) | 90,720 | 105,105 | 187,812 |\n| UniiChat on pi, 5-minute (paper) | 76,109 | 94,433 | 163,894 |\n| Continued session, 1-hour (round 1, measured) | 76,297 | 81,040 | 119,060 |\n| Continued session, 5-minute (repriced) | 66,678 | 71,122 | 107,072 |\n| Round-1 ratio | 1.45x | 1.54x | 1.84x |\n| pi 5-minute vs continued as measured | 1.00x | 1.17x | 1.38x |\n| pi 5-minute vs continued at the same price | 1.14x | 1.33x | 1.53x |\n\n**5. Compactions (Haiku xhigh), Sonnet chat, 49 sends:** cost 4,393,769 in Haiku token units, 96% of it output including thinking. The pi shape removes 527 input tokens per send, giving 4,317,923 (-1.7%, paper).", "findings": ["Measured, contradicts round 1: the 1-hour write at 2x is not forced by Claude Code. `CLAUDE_CODE_PROMPT_CACHE_TTL=5m` (also `FORCE_PROMPT_CACHING_5M` and a `promptCacheTtl` setting) makes all four marks 5-minute. On the subscription every written token then came back as a 5-minute write: 41,292 tokens over 24 Haiku turns and 14,460 over 8 Sonnet turns, with token counts identical to round 1.", "The trial core itself hard-codes `ttl: \"1h\"` on the view mark (`claude_cli.py`, `blocks_of`). Round 1 listed 5-minute marks as not tested, yet concluded the lifetime could not be chosen.", "Measured: the owner's hypothesis is right for stock Claude Code and mostly irrelevant to round 1. A stock call with his normal configuration carries 27,295 input tokens before any content (n=1); round 1 ran a thinned Claude Code at 2,253. What Claude Code still adds there is two blocks, 527 tokens per call: a 170-token reminder before the view and a 357-token environment block after the message.", "Round 1 reported those two blocks as about 240 and 356 tokens; I measured 170 and 357 by difference (n=1 each). Round 1's stock figure was 28,793; mine is 27,295 (n=1, empty cwd).", "The environment block costs more than its size suggests. It sits after the message, so it is written again on every turn: 357 of about 1,720 written tokens per warm Haiku turn in E2, or 21% of each turn's cache write.", "Pi facts (no calls). `omp` is 18.10.1, a compiled binary at `~/.local/share/tern/omp/versions/18.10.1`. The pi-ai SDK source on disk is 18.9.2, at `/mnt/zer0models/home-offload/kvn/bun/install/global/node_modules/@oh-my-pi/pi-ai`. The captured shapes are from 18.9.2, not the binary's version.", "Credential type: OMP's Anthropic credential is a subscription OAuth login, not an API key. It is one row in `~/.omp/agent/agent.db`, table `auth_credentials`, `credential_type` oauth, with an OAuth-class access token plus refresh and expiry fields. No `ANTHROPIC_*` variable is set and there is no Anthropic API-key row. Per the SDK source, environment `ANTHROPIC_OAUTH_TOKEN` then `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` would be used if set (`registry/hooks/env.ts`), and the request shape is chosen by token form (`utils/anthropic-auth.ts`). No key material was printed or copied.", "Under that login pi-ai does not send [tools][system][view][message]. It sends the Claude Code billing header with a computed `cch` hash, the Claude Code identity line, tools renamed `_zoom` and `_date`, a `claude-cli/2.1.280` user agent and `claude-code-20250219` in the beta header. The source calls these values 'spoofed' and 'cloaking'. The gist's exact request comes out only with an API key.", "The caller does control everything else in pi-ai: the system prompt (after the two forced blocks), the tool list, message blocks, all four cache marks and their lifetime through `onPayload` or `cacheRetention`, and effort through `reasoning` (thinking is disabled when it is not passed). Usage comes back as input, output, cache read and cache write, with a 5-minute/1-hour split in `usage.cttl`.", "Pi's default lifetime on a subscription login is also 1 hour, by design ('match Claude Code's native policy'). Running on pi would not have changed the write price unless `cacheRetention: \"short\"` was passed.", "Unmeasured risk on pi: the billing hash in system[0] changes on every request and sits inside the system-prompt cache prefix. If Anthropic does not strip that block before caching, the whole view would be rewritten on every call. Real Claude Code sends the same kind of header, so this probably works, but I did not test it.", "Decomposition of round 1's extra turn cost (repriced from round-1 rows; pi columns are paper). Sonnet, 26 turns, extra 33,963 units: dropping the two blocks removes 19,540, pricing writes at 1.25x removes 21,700, both together 34,151, which is all of it against the continued session as measured.", "Like for like (both arms at 1.25x) the Sonnet gap is 9,431 units, or 1.14x. Of that, 6,785 is the UniiChat arm's extra output (6,509 against 5,152 tokens); the input side is 6% above the continued session.", "Haiku, 26 turns: the like-for-like gap is 23,311 units (1.33x). Extra output alone is 29,795 units (11,170 against 5,211 tokens), so the input side is 14% below the continued session. Scaled Haiku, 42 turns: gap 56,822 (1.53x), extra output 52,885, input side 5% above.", "So once the harness blocks and the 2x price are removed, the input side is within about 15% of a continued session at 26-42 messages. What remains is output: fresh-view turns write longer replies and, in the scaled chat, make zoom calls (53 requests against 48).", "Compaction cost is untouched by the harness question. In the Sonnet chat it was 96% output tokens (Haiku xhigh thinking); the pi shape would trim 1.7%. Round 1's 5x and 58x with compactions stand on any harness.", "Measured, n=1: a 5-minute entry was gone after a 330-second pause and the whole view was written again. Inferred from the measured sizes: at an 80 KB view a cold rewrite costs about 41,900 units at 1.25x, while 1-hour entries cost about 1,000-1,300 units more per warm turn. One-hour entries are therefore cheaper once more than roughly 1 turn in 32-41 follows a 5-60 minute pause. For a chat a person types into, the gist's 'don't buy 1-hour entries' is likely the wrong advice.", "Mid-view change is the same on either harness with the gist's two marks: one changed line rewrote the whole 64 KB view (27,034 tokens), identical at 5 minutes and 1 hour. Pi's spare marks could in principle pin an older part of the view through a batch; I did not test that.", "Compactions sharing the turns' tools-and-system entry when both are Haiku: already true on Claude Code in round 1 (a cold compaction read the 1,641-token entry). Pi adds nothing here, and I made no pi measurement of it.", "My own side effect, repaired: the pi-ai SDK wrote 11 request dumps to `~/.omp/logs/http-400-requests/` and 2 log files to `~/.omp/logs/` during the sink capture. They held only dummy-token requests to 127.0.0.1. I checked they were mine by PID and timestamp and deleted them; the directory, which predates this run, is left in place and empty.", "The stock call ran once with the owner's normal configuration, so his plugins and hooks ran once. It ended at `--max-turns 1` with a tool call pending (one request, 357 output tokens). I did not inspect what his hooks wrote.", "The trial repo head is now 098433d (background compactor), committed by another round-2 experiment, not by me."], "decision_or_answer": "**Most of round 1's extra turn cost was the harness and its settings, not the design, and you do not need pi to remove most of it.**\n\n- **How much was harness:** for Sonnet at 26 turns the 1.45x came from two things in about equal parts. Claude Code's two added blocks (527 tokens per call, 357 of them rewritten every turn) account for 19,540 of the 33,963 extra units. Pricing writes at 2x instead of 1.25x accounts for 21,700. Together they cover all of it.\n- **The 2x price was a setting:** `CLAUDE_CODE_PROMPT_CACHE_TTL=5m` gives 5-minute writes on the subscription, measured this round. That alone takes Sonnet from 1.45x to 1.16x against the continued session as measured.\n- **What is inherent at short lengths:** with both arms at the same price, UniiChat is still 1.14x (Sonnet), 1.33x (Haiku) and 1.53x (scaled, 42 turns). Nearly all of that is extra output tokens from fresh-view turns and zoom calls; the input side is within about 15% either way.\n- **Compactions are unaffected:** they are 96% thinking output on Haiku xhigh, so the 5x-58x figure does not move on pi. Only a cheaper summarizer changes that.\n- **Does pi change the conclusion:** on paper, pi with 5-minute marks reaches 1.00x of the continued session as measured for Sonnet, 1.17x for Haiku and 1.38x scaled. That is parity at best at these lengths, and still no saving. The crossover question still needs the long replay.\n- **Stock Claude Code bloat is real but was not in round 1:** 27,295 tokens per call with your normal configuration against about 1,726 on pi, roughly 16x. Round 1 ran a thinned Claude Code at 2,253.\n\n**Decision for you:** whether to run the live pi replay. The harness is not built; the capture script and cases are. It would send up to 400 requests on your subscription through a client that identifies itself to Anthropic as Claude Code with a computed attestation. It would confirm two things the paper cannot: that the per-request billing hash does not break the system-prompt cache, and the real usage numbers. I would not expect it to change the answer above.\n\nWhatever you decide on pi, the cheap next step is to make the view mark's lifetime a parameter in the core and rerun with 5-minute marks.", "core_changes": "None by me: no commit, no edit under the trial repo.\n\n- **5-minute arm:** done in the E6 harness only. `e6lib.set_ttl(\"5m\")` swaps `claude_cli.blocks_of` in-process so the view mark carries no `ttl`, and the call passes `CLAUDE_CODE_PROMPT_CACHE_TTL=5m` in the child environment.\n- **Suggested core change, not made:** `blocks_of` hard-codes `ttl=\"1h\"` and `Session._content` calls it with the default. Make the lifetime a `ClaudeCLI` parameter, record it in each row, and pass the matching environment variable. Note that `child_env` strips `CLAUDE*` variables, so the variable has to go through the per-call `env`.\n- **Repo head:** now 098433d, committed by another round-2 experiment. My runs imported the core at whatever head was checked out at the time; only `claude_cli.py`, `prompt.py` and `turn.tools_config` were used.", "raw_files": "Everything is under `/mnt/zer0models/z0-wt/salvage/optchat-trial/results/E6/`.\n\n- `analysis.txt`, `analysis.json` - all tables above; open `analysis.txt` first\n- `capture/shapes.txt` - block-by-block summary of every captured request\n- `capture/cc-requests.jsonl`, `capture/cc5m-requests.jsonl`, `capture/cc5mforce-requests.jsonl` - Claude Code bodies at the sink (email replaced, auth header reduced to a class label)\n- `capture/pi-requests.jsonl`, `capture/pi-cases.json` - pi-ai bodies for 11 variants (dummy tokens, scrubbed from the cases file)\n- `thin/`, `turns5m/`, `mid5m/`, `gap5m/` - `index.jsonl` (one row per call) and `calls.jsonl` (flags, usage by class, wall time, model, raw events)\n- `stock/usage.jsonl` - the one stock call, usage only\n- `logs/turns-mid-5m.log`, `logs/gap5m.log`\n- `harness/` - `sink.py`, `capture.py`, `pi_capture.ts`, `e6lib.py`, `e6_cc.py`, `analyze_e6.py`, `shape.py`\n\nShared ledger: `/mnt/zer0models/z0-wt/salvage/optchat-trial/results/ledger-r2.jsonl` (experiment names starting `E6-`).\n\nRound-1 inputs read: `results/E2/index-turns.jsonl`, `results/E2/index-mid.jsonl`, `results/E3/{sonnet,haiku,scaled}-A/calls.jsonl`, `results/E3/{sonnet,haiku}-B/calls.jsonl`.", "limits": "- **Every pi number is inferred.** It is measured Claude Code usage minus the two blocks, repriced. No request went through pi to Anthropic.\n- **The paper model assumes** pi's tools-and-system segment costs the same 1,641 tokens (names and identity line differ by about 10 bytes), that Anthropic ignores the changing billing hash for caching, and that a 5-minute mark is honoured on pi's route as it was on Claude Code's.\n- **Block sizes are n=1 each**, measured by adding the block's text to a message. The email was a placeholder of similar length, so the 170 could be off by a few tokens.\n- **Stock overhead is n=1**, on Haiku, in an empty directory with no project files. A real project session will differ.\n- **E3 was repriced, not rerun.** The 5-minute rows for E3 assume the same token counts as at 1 hour (true 32 of 32 times in E2) and no pause over 5 minutes between turns. Round 1's serial compaction waits were often longer than that, so a real 5-minute run of that harness would have paid cold rewrites.\n- **The stripping rule for multi-request turns** (environment block written once per turn, read on later requests) is an assumption from the captured shape, not measured.\n- **The continued-session baseline was left as measured.** It also carries the reminder block; on pi it would save roughly 1,400 units over 26 turns, which I ignored.\n- **The cost unit weighs output at 5x and a 1-hour write at 2x.** On a subscription no price is observable, so these are list-price weights, not money.\n- **5-minute expiry is one sample at 330 s.** Nothing between 20 s and 330 s was tested.\n- **One run per arm, one corpus.** The output-token differences that make up the remaining gap come from single round-1 runs and carry no variance estimate.\n- **Captured pi shapes are from SDK source 18.9.2**; the installed `omp` binary is 18.10.1 and may differ.\n- **Not tested:** a spare mark surviving a batch, compactions on pi, 16-line blocks, any chat past 42 turns."}}
7 dict_keys(['type', 'key', 'agentId', 'result'])
{"type": "result", "key": "v2:5cfe2a55505d50aaaee6d2a96f50bf3e5c73919bcd76cfaedb19e4952117e1d2", "agentId": "a1d9a542274fd0b66", "result": {"experiment": "E5, the long replay: UniiChat (arm A, fresh call per message, gist prompt, view at 128,000/64,000, compaction view 32,000/16,000, Haiku-low background compactor up to 8 at once) against one continued Claude Code session (arm B, thinned as in round 1, auto-compaction untouched), both on Sonnet, same 260-turn synthetic engineering script on a scratch Python repository; then Opus late questions from the Sonnet end state; plus a stock-session arm B2.", "completed": "Partly. Both arms ran 112 of the 260 scripted turns, equally, and stopped there because E5's Haiku allowance ran out (2,387 of 2,500 used; the stop rule kept 130 back). That is past three real 128 KB batches (turns 52, 77, 103).\n\n- Arm A logged 1,161 messages and built 2,318 tree nodes up to level 10.\n- Opus phase: complete, 10 turns per arm (12 recall questions, 8 compliance probes), 40 Opus requests.\n- B2 (stock session): 37 of 40 turns, stopped by the Sonnet allowance (643 of 650).\n- Not reached: turns 113-260, so the 150/200/250 rows are empty.\n- Auto-compaction in arm B never fired: Claude Code gives Sonnet a 1,000,000-token window and the session ended at 344,607 tokens.\n- One interruption: arm A halted at turn 37 when one line failed three times in a row; I raised the retry limit to 12 with a high-effort fallback after six, and resumed. The fallback was never used.\n- I counted the caps in API requests, as the round-1 ledger does, not in `claude -p` invocations.\n- One run per arm, so no variance estimate.", "calls_made": "E5 totals from the shared ledger: Haiku 2,387 of 2,500, Sonnet 643 of 650, Opus 40 of 80. No rate-limit or usage-limit error occurred.\n\n- Sonnet: arm A 301 requests over 112 turns, arm B 247, B2 95 over 37 turns.\n- Haiku: 2,383 compaction sends in the main run, plus 4 in a tool-permission smoke test.\n- Opus: arm A 23 requests, arm B 17.\n- No pi-route or local-model calls were made in this experiment.", "results_table": "Cost unit = input + W x cache write + 0.1 x cache read + 5 x output. \"A+comp\" adds Haiku compaction units scaled by 0.0627, the CLI's own Haiku/Sonnet price ratio. USD is the CLI estimate. All figures measured, n = turns shown, one run.\n\n| Turn | Cum. unit W2: A turns / A+comp / B | Cum. unit W1.25: A / A+comp / B | Cum. USD: A / A+comp / B | A view bytes | A messages | B prompt tokens | Requests A / B / Haiku sends |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 25 | 336,881 / 479,210 / 342,472 | 278,340 / 401,264 / 306,312 | 0.59 / 0.81 / 0.52 | 47,472 | 172 | 48,215 | 62 / 62 / 460 |\n| 50 | 1,033,920 / 1,419,704 / 1,038,436 | 879,269 / 1,210,674 / 955,906 | 1.69 / 2.31 / 1.49 | 119,304 | 482 | 110,042 | 129 / 117 / 1,124 |\n| 100 | 2,498,064 / 3,259,896 / 3,575,688 | 2,149,592 / 2,800,926 / 3,371,287 | 3.99 / 5.21 / 4.71 | 125,818 | 1,040 | 272,537 | 261 / 221 / 2,137 |\n| 112 (end) | 2,911,371 / 3,771,656 / 4,611,482 | 2,501,525 / 3,237,351 / 4,353,029 | 4.65 / 6.02 / 6.00 | 94,040 | 1,161 | 344,607 | 301 / 247 / 2,383 |\n| 150, 200, 250 | not reached | | | | | | |\n\nMean unit (W2) per turn, by stretch:\n\n| Turns | A turn call | A compaction (scaled) | B |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1-25 | 13,475 | 5,697 | 13,699 |\n| 26-50 | 27,882 | 9,744 | 27,839 |\n| 51-75 | 27,300 | 6,465 | 40,592 |\n| 76-100 | 31,266 | 8,585 | 60,898 |\n| 101-112 | 34,442 | 8,209 | 86,316 |\n\nCrossover (first turn from which A stays at or under B to the end):\n\n| Measure | Per turn, 10-turn mean: turn calls / with compactions | Cumulative: turn calls / with compactions |\n|---|---|---|\n| Unit W1.25 | 48 / 57 | 32 / 80 |\n| Unit W2.0 | 49 / 58 | 57 / 89 |\n| CLI USD | 57 / 84 | 80 / not reached (6.02 vs 6.00 at 112) |\n\nBatches in arm A:\n\n| Turn | Pairs merged | View sent that turn | View after | Next turn cache write / read (tokens) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 52 | 348 | 124,266 | 65,652 | 30,254 / 4,227 |\n| 77 | 263 | 118,428 | 67,467 | 33,138 / 4,227 |\n| 103 | 276 | 127,752 | 65,634 | 34,647 / 114,798 |\n\nWall time over 112 turns:\n\n| | Arm A | Arm B |\n|---|---|---|\n| Turn calls | 1,275 s (11.4 s mean) | 1,462 s (13.1 s mean) |\n| Wait for the view if the next message came at once | 850 s total; mean 7.6, median 3.8, p90 19.4, max 95.0 s | none |\n| Wait actually paid at turn start in this lockstep run | 116 s | none |\n| Background compaction | 2,792 s busy, 6,533 call-seconds, 2.74 s per send | none |\n\nCorrectness and compliance (Sonnet, 112 turns):\n\n| | Arm A | Arm B |\n|---|---|---|\n| Recall questions (turn 60 on) | 50 of 54 | 54 of 54 |\n| Recall of corrections | 13 of 16 | 16 of 16 |\n| Edit turns where nothing was changed | 5 of 73 | 0 of 73 |\n| Standing-order checks on edit turns, gradable | 351 of 351 | 383 of 383 |\n| Checks not gradable (no change made / shell refused) | 31 / 4 | 2 / 1 |\n| Trap checks in probe turns | 27 of 27 | 27 of 27 |\n| Bulk 44-file answers | 2 of 2 | 2 of 2 |\n\nOpus late phase (10 turns per arm):\n\n| | Arm A | Arm B |\n|---|---|---|\n| Recall | 11 of 12 | 12 of 12 |\n| Trap checks | 7 of 7 gradable (1 not gradable) | 8 of 8 |\n| Requests | 23 | 17 |\n| Unit W2 | 289,820 | 7,253,227 |\n\nB2, stock session, turns 1-37:\n\n| | B2 stock | B thinned | A turn calls |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| First request prompt tokens | 23,690 | 4,852 | 4,227 prefix + view |\n| Requests | 95 | 88 | 95 |\n| Unit W2 | 889,826 | 625,676 | 667,964 |\n| CLI USD | 1.25 | 0.93 | 1.13 (1.55 with compactions) |", "findings": ["Measured: the cost crossover exists on turn calls. Arm A's turn cost flattens at about 27,000-34,000 units per turn once the view saw-tooths, while arm B's climbs with its prompt (13,699 to 86,316 per turn, prompt 4,852 to 344,607 tokens). Per turn, A is cheaper from about turn 48-49; cumulatively from turn 57 (W2) or 32 (W1.25). n = 112 turns, one run.", "Measured: with Haiku-low compactions counted at the CLI's own prices, the per-turn crossover moves to turn 57-58 and the cumulative one to turn 80-89 in units; in CLI USD the two arms are level at turn 112 (6.02 vs 6.00). Round 1's 5x-58x compaction penalty is gone at low effort: compaction is 23% of arm A's USD (1.37 of 6.02).", "Inferred, and it matters: the CLI prices Haiku at 0.0627 of Sonnet per unit. Compaction used 13.7M Haiku units (W2), 4.7 times arm A's turn units. At a one-third price ratio arm A would total about 7.5M units against B's 4.6M, with no crossover by turn 112.", "Measured, contradicts the gist's premise for this setup: the continued session never auto-compacted and never forgot. Sonnet has a 1,000,000-token window here; B answered 54 of 54 recall questions and broke no standing order. The gist's failure mode did not appear in 112 tool-heavy turns; what did appear is linear cost growth.", "Measured, new failure in arm A: the Sonnet turn sometimes answers the user's message as if it were a compaction, writing a summary line ('user (msg 323): ... Not started, no edits') instead of acting. This happened on 12 of 112 turns: 9 of the 13 order and correction messages, 3 of the 7 fact messages reached, and 1 task; four more tasks came back as status lines of the same kind. In all, 5 of 73 edit turns were not carried out (35, 49, 50, 59, 80); arm B had none.", "Measured consequence: the rounding correction at turn 50 was never applied in arm A's repository, and A later reported it as 'on hold' both on Sonnet (turn 99) and on Opus. That is the one substantive recall error; the other 3 misses are one turn (105) that spent all 8 requests on zoom calls and was cut off.", "Measured: where arm A did act, it obeyed every standing order and later correction, including orders 50-100 turns old sitting in level 5-10 lines: 351 of 351 gradable checks and 27 of 27 trap checks. Low-effort lines did not lose the orders within this horizon, against E4's warning.", "Measured: zoom was hardly used. 12 calls on 4 of 112 turns, all on recall questions, none on a task or probe; the 512-byte lines were enough for the rest.", "Measured: Haiku at low effort tries to call tools during compaction far more often in a tool-heavy chat than E4 saw. 266 of 2,383 sends (11.2%) failed that way (Bash 165, zoom 102, date 4, Edit 1, Read 1), against 4 of 171 in E4. Each failure is a wasted send and delays the line; this is what stalled turn 37. Sends per model-built node were 2.12; 11 of 2,318 nodes ended over 512 bytes.", "Measured: the sawtooth behaves as the gist says. Three batches merged 263-348 pairs and took the view from 118-128 KB down to 64-67 KB; the largest view sent was 127,752 bytes. The turn after a batch rewrote the whole view: 30,254-34,647 tokens of cache write.", "Measured, confirms round 1's lookback finding: after each 44-file turn (90-95 new lines) the next turn rewrote the whole view (30,515 and 33,138 tokens written, only the 4,227-token prefix read).", "Measured cache-read share of all input tokens: arm A turns 95.6%, compactions 93.0%, arm B 98.9%. The gist's 98.6% and 96.2% were not reached, and the continued session caches better than UniiChat.", "Measured: 'the wait takes seconds' holds at low effort on the median (3.8 s) but not in the tail (p90 19.4 s, max 95 s after a 44-file turn). Turn calls themselves were slightly faster in arm A (11.4 s vs 13.1 s mean).", "Measured: Claude Code's stock overhead is about 18,800 tokens per request over the thinned session (23,690 vs 4,852 on the first request, 21 tools vs 4). Over 37 turns that made the stock session 42% dearer in units than the thinned one (889,826 vs 625,676) and 33% dearer than arm A's turn calls. This supports the owner's point that the harness adds a constant per-request load; a stock baseline would move the turn-call crossover earlier.", "Measured, a design artefact rather than a UniiChat result: on Opus, arm B cost 25 times arm A (7.25M vs 0.29M units), because each forked question re-wrote the 347,000-token session to the Opus cache. A real model switch would pay that once, not ten times.", "Harness artefact to discount: my shell allow list refused compound commands with heredocs (7 of 458 tool calls in A, 5 of 244 in B). In 4 A turns and 1 B turn the agent then did not run `make verify` and said so; I graded those 'blocked', not failed.", "Arm A worked differently from arm B on the same tasks: 458 tool calls against 244 and 301 requests against 247. Under the gist prompt it leaned on shell commands; under Claude Code's prompt it used Read and Edit."], "decision_or_answer": "UniiChat through Claude Code breaks even with a continued session at roughly turn 50-60 on turn calls and turn 80-90 with compactions, and is clearly cheaper per turn after that (about 2.5x by turn 110). It does not yet buy better correctness: the continued session was perfect here, while UniiChat skipped 5 of 73 tasks.\n\nWhat appeared from the gist's economics:\n- Constant-size context: the view stayed within 64-128 KB over 1,161 messages.\n- Flat per-turn cost against a linearly growing baseline.\n- The batch sawtooth, with one full view rewrite per batch.\n- Cheap compaction, once effort is low.\n\nWhat did not appear:\n- The baseline never forgot or auto-compacted, because the window is 1M tokens.\n- \"Nearly every token from cache\" was weaker than the baseline (95.6% vs 98.9%).\n- Zoom was almost unused.\n- \"The wait takes seconds\" fails in the tail (up to 95 s).\n- The single shared prompt confuses turns with compactions on 12 of 112 turns.\n\nThe conclusion on total cost depends on the Haiku price: at the CLI's ratio the arms are level at turn 112; at a one-third ratio UniiChat is still about 1.6x dearer.\n\nWhether a leaner harness changes this is the pi arm's question. E5's evidence is that the stock Claude Code session carries about 18,800 extra tokens per request.", "core_changes": "One commit on trial/optchat-uniichat, 098433d (on top of fd454e7), local only, no push. 43 tests pass (40 existing unchanged, 3 new).\n\n- `compact.Background`: a thread in the chat's own process that reruns the compactor whenever a node is ready, so lines and merges are built while the turn call runs. `run_turn(background=...)` waits only for the lines before its message.\n- `Compactor.poll`: a run used to block on its oldest in-flight call and miss messages appended meanwhile; with a background compactor it now looks at the queues every 0.05 s.\n- `Chat.settle()`, called by `run_turn` after the wait and before render: runs a due batch on real line sizes. Without it, a turn of many long messages could leave the view far past 128,000 bytes and the next turn was refused. Which pairs merge is unchanged. In this run all three batches fired at an append, so this path was never the trigger.\n\nNot fixed: the placeholder-size accounting inside `append` itself. Its effect was small here: batches ended at 65.6-67.5 KB, and 1 of 2,383 compaction contexts exceeded 32,000 bytes (32,362).\n\nHarness choices outside the core:\n- The arms' working directory is the scratch repository, which has no git metadata so the request prefix stays stable.\n- The compactor's slack is 16,000 bytes.\n- The user instructions ask for independent tool calls to be issued together.\n- The commit reuses the branch's existing author identity through environment variables; no git config was changed.", "raw_files": "Everything is under /mnt/zer0models/z0-wt/salvage/optchat-trial/results/E5/.\n\n- analysis.txt and analysis.json: per-turn table, totals, crossover, batches, grades, every graded item.\n- script.json: the 260 turns, the Opus questions and the key.\n- A/, B/, B2/, opus-A/, opus-B/: turns.jsonl (one row per turn, with steps and file changes) and calls.jsonl (one row per call, with flags, usage by class and wall time).\n- halts.jsonl, run-main.log, run-opus.log, run-b2.log: why and where each run stopped.\n- seed/: repository snapshots and arm A's final view used for the Opus phase.\n- harness/: mkrepo.py, script.py, run.py, analyze.py.\n\nElsewhere under /mnt/zer0models/z0-wt/salvage/optchat-trial/:\n- results/ledger-r2.jsonl: the shared ledger.\n- chats/e5-A: arm A's log, tree and view.\n- scratch/e5-A-repo, scratch/e5-B-repo, scratch/e5-B2-repo: the arms' repositories.\n- repo: the core, at 098433d.", "limits": "- Length: 112 of 260 turns, one run per arm, one script, one account with both arms and up to 8 compactions running at once, so wall times are not isolated. Recall and probe turns only start at turn 60: 18 recall turns (54 questions) and 17 probe turns were reached.\n- Baseline never stressed: no auto-compaction and no context rot were observed, so the gist's main claim (better behaviour than a compacted or overlong session) remains untested. Reaching the 1M-token window would take roughly 300+ turns of this script.\n- Prices: USD and the Haiku scaling come from the CLI's own estimate for these model ids (Haiku about 0.10 USD and Sonnet about 1.6 USD per million units, as fitted from its figures). I did not verify them against a price list. The CLI's cost figure is cumulative within a process and a resumed session; I took differences.\n- Compactor effort was low only; the high-effort arm E4 suggested for deep standing orders was not run.\n- Grading is by regex and file inspection against the key, with no human or model judge. A check counts only when the turn changed files, so arm A's five undone turns show up as 'not carried out', not as compliance failures.\n- Synthetic data only. No email address is stored in any E5 file (scanned). Arm B's sessions are saved by Claude Code under the owner's config directory, as in round 1; I did not read anything there.\n- Tool scope: Read, Edit and Write were limited to each arm's repository and the shell to a command allow list. One arm A Edit named a path outside the repository and changed nothing. python3 was allowed, so the scope is by rule and instruction, not a sandbox.\n- No processes are left running. The repositories were restored to their end-of-Sonnet state after the Opus phase."}}
