      "measured": "E4: the Sonnet reader made 29-52 zoom calls per 40 questions and scored 38-40. E5: zoom was called 12 times on 4 of 112 turns, all recall questions; one turn spent all 8 requests zooming and was cut off (3 of the 4 recall misses).",
      "verdict": "works when asked a question; hardly used during real work"
    },
    {
      "claim": "One system prompt serves turns and compactions (s5)",
      "measured": "E5: on 12 of 112 turns the Sonnet turn replied with a summary line instead of acting (turns 2, 4, 6, 7, 9, 11, 12, 23, 29, 35, 89, 101; my own narrower regex finds 9 of them). 5 of 73 edit turns changed nothing (35, 49, 50, 59, 80); the baseline had 0. One correction (turn 50) was never applied and was later reported as 'on hold' on both Sonnet and Opus.",
      "verdict": "new failure, not anticipated by the gist"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The wait before a turn takes seconds (s6)",
      "measured": "E5 with a background compactor at low effort: wait if the next message came at once, median 3.8 s, mean 7.6 s, p90 19.4 s, max 95.0 s (n=112). Round 1 at xhigh, serial: 0 on 19 of 26 turns, otherwise median 23-63 s, max 296 s.",
      "verdict": "holds on the median at low effort, not in the tail"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Compactions share the turns' cache entry for tools and system prompt (s4)",
      "measured": "True only when turns and compactions use the same model (round 1, Haiku with Haiku). In E5 turns were Sonnet and compactions Haiku, so nothing was shared. Sharing also means listing the turn's tools to the compactor, which is what produced the 11.2% tool-call rate.",
      "verdict": "holds only for same-model setups, and has a cost"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Up to 8 compactions at once, in the background (s4)",
      "measured": "Not in the round-1 core. Added in commit 098433d (local, 3 new tests, 43 pass as reported; I did not rerun them). E5: 6,533 call-seconds of compaction in 2,792 s busy time.",
      "verdict": "works after a core change"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Long sessions rot and get compacted; UniiChat avoids that (s1)",
      "measured": "The continued session grew to 344,607 prompt tokens over 112 tool-heavy turns with 0 compaction events and 0 errors: 54 of 54 recall, 383 of 383 compliance checks, 27 of 27 traps. Context window reported by the CLI: 1,000,000.",
      "verdict": "not observed; untested, not refuted"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Implied: this is cheaper than continuing a session",
      "measured": "Round 1, 26-42 messages: 1.45-1.84x dearer on turn calls. E5, 112 turns: cheaper on turn calls from turn 32-57; with compactions from turn 82-89 in units and level at 112 in CLI USD. See economics.",
      "verdict": "depends on length and on two prices; holds past roughly 60-90 tool-heavy turns under the gist's own price weights"
    }
  ],
  "economics": "All figures recomputed from the call rows unless marked. Unit = input + W x cache write + 0.1 x cache read + 5 x output. One run per arm, Sonnet turns, 112 turns each, arm B is a thinned Claude Code session.\n\n**End state at turn 112**\n\n| Measure | UniiChat turn calls | UniiChat + compactions | Continued session |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Units, 1-hour writes (W=2), Haiku scaled 0.0627 as E5 did | 2,911,371 | 3,772,166 | 4,611,482 |\n| Same, Haiku scaled 0.05 (the CLI's real 1:20) | 2,911,371 | 3,597,811 | 4,611,482 |\n| Units, W=1.25 (repriced, not rerun) | 2,501,525 | 3,088,656 to 3,237,788 | 4,353,029 |\n| CLI USD | 4.65 | 6.02 | 5.99 |\n\n**Crossover: first turn from which UniiChat stays at or below the baseline to the end**\n\n| Pricing | Turn calls only | With compactions |\n|---|---|---|\n| Units W=2, Haiku at 0.0627 | 57 | 89 |\n| Units W=2, Haiku at 0.05 | 57 | 82 |\n| Units W=1.25, Haiku at 0.05 | 32 | 71 |\n| CLI USD (reads at 0.05x for Sonnet) | 80 | not reached by 112 |\n| Units, Haiku at one third of Sonnet | 57 or 32 | not reached (7.49M against 4.61M) |\n\nPer-turn (10-turn mean) crossover: turn 48-49 on turn calls, 57-58 with compactions in units; 57 and 84 in USD.\n\n**What drives it**\n- The continued session's cost per turn grows with its prompt: 13,699 units per turn in turns 1-25, 86,316 in turns 101-112. UniiChat's turn calls stay at 27,300-34,442 from turn 26 on.\n- Compaction used 13.73M Haiku units, 4.7x the arm's own turn units, in 2,383 sends. It is affordable only because Haiku is priced at one twentieth of Sonnet by the CLI. It was 1.37 of the arm's 6.02 USD (23%).\n- The baseline is almost all cache reads: 32.28M of its 32.62M input tokens. So the read price decides the result. At 0.1x the baseline costs 4.61M units; at the CLI's 0.05x it costs the equivalent of 3.00M and UniiChat with compactions is level (3.01M).\n- By turns 101-112 UniiChat is 2.5x cheaper per turn on turn calls and 2.0x with compactions (E5 quoted the 2.5x without saying it excluded compactions).\n\n**Short chats (round 1, repriced in E6)**\n- 26 turns: 1.45x (Sonnet) and 1.54x (Haiku) dearer on turn calls at 1-hour writes; 42 turns scaled: 1.84x.\n- With both arms at 5-minute writes: 1.14x, 1.33x, 1.53x. Most of the rest is extra output tokens from fresh-view turns.\n- Round 1's 5x-58x with compactions came from xhigh effort. E4's corrected per-line cost: 0.00657 USD at xhigh against 0.00135 at low.\n\n**Against a stock session (B2, 37 turns only):** stock 889,826 units, thinned 625,676, UniiChat turn calls 667,964. In USD: 1.25, 0.93, and 1.13 (1.55 with compactions).\n\n**Inferred, not measured:** any figure past turn 112; the Opus economics (the 25x in E5's Opus phase is an artefact of forking a 347,000-token session ten times).",
  "harness_bloat_answer": "**Is Claude Code's harness bloated? Yes in its stock form. Did that cause round 1's result? No. Would pi run it completely differently? Not on the evidence so far, and pi itself was not run.**\n\nMeasured overhead per request, before any content:\n- Stock Claude Code with the owner's normal configuration: 27,295 tokens (E6, n=1, Haiku, empty directory).\n- Stock Claude Code tools and skills without user settings: 23,690 tokens on the first request (E5 arm B2; 21 tools, 17 skills).\n- Thinned Claude Code as used in both rounds: 2,253 tokens (1,641 for tools and system, plus a 170-token reminder block and a 357-token environment block, each n=1 by difference). I recomputed all three from the rows.\n- pi-ai on the subscription login: about 1,726 tokens. This is inferred (2,253 minus the two blocks); no request went through pi.\n\nWhat the bloat costs:\n- It is a fixed block that is cached after the first request. Over 37 turns the stock session cost 42% more units than the thinned one (889,826 against 625,676, n=37 turns, one run).\n- UniiChat replaces the system prompt, so it never carries that block. Against a stock session it is therefore ahead earlier: already 25% cheaper on turn calls at turn 37, though still dearer in USD once compactions are added (1.55 against 1.25).\n- It does not explain round 1. Round 1's 1.45x came about half from the two small blocks (527 tokens per call, 357 of them rewritten every turn) and half from 1-hour cache writes at 2x, which E6 showed is a setting, not a constraint.\n\nWhat pi would change, on paper only:\n- At 26-42 turns, pi with 5-minute marks comes to 1.00x (Sonnet), 1.17x (Haiku), 1.38x (scaled) of the continued session as measured. That is parity at best.\n- It would not change the long-run shape: flat turn cost against a linearly growing session is a property of the design, not the harness.\n- It would not change compaction cost, which is 93-96% output and thinking tokens.\n- It might help with two things nobody tested: pinning an older part of the view through a batch with a spare cache mark, and keeping tools visible to the compactor without letting them run.\n\nWhy pi was not run: OMP's Anthropic credential is the subscription OAuth login (I confirmed the type column only: one anthropic row, type oauth; no ANTHROPIC_* variable set). So the rule allowed it. But under that login pi-ai does not send the gist's request: the captured shape shows a billing header with a per-request hash, the line \"You are Claude Code, Anthropic's official CLI for Claude.\", and a claude-cli user agent. E6 held back because that presents a third-party client to Anthropic as Claude Code on the owner's account. This is the one open decision for the owner.",
  "correctness_and_compliance": "**The baseline never auto-compacted, so the comparison the brief asked for could not be made.** Arm B's 112 rows show 0 compaction events, one session id throughout, a 1,000,000-token window, and a last prompt of 344,607 tokens. E5 estimates 300+ turns of this script to fill the window (inferred).\n\nSonnet, 112 turns, one run, graded by regex and file inspection (I took the grades from E5's analysis.json; I did not rerun the grader):\n\n| | UniiChat | Continued session |\n|---|---|---|\n| Recall questions (from turn 60) | 50 of 54 | 54 of 54 |\n| Recall of corrections | 13 of 16 | 16 of 16 |\n| Edit turns where nothing was changed | 5 of 73 | 0 of 73 |\n| Standing-order checks, gradable | 351 of 351 | 383 of 383 |\n| Not gradable (no change / shell refused) | 31 / 4 | 2 / 1 |\n| Trap checks in probe turns | 27 of 27 | 27 of 27 |\n| Turns answered as a compaction line | 12 of 112 | not applicable |\n\nOpus late phase, 10 turns per arm: recall 11 of 12 against 12 of 12; traps 7 of 7 gradable against 8 of 8.\n\nReading these:\n- UniiChat's losses are not memory losses in the gist's sense. Three of the four recall misses are one turn that used all 8 requests on zoom calls. The fourth is a correction the agent never applied because it replied with a summary line, then reported as 'on hold' 49 turns later and again on Opus.\n- When UniiChat acted, it broke no order, including ones sitting in level 5-10 lines. E4's warning that low-effort lines drop standing rules at depth (9 of 13 left at level 5) did not turn into a behavioural failure within 112 turns.\n- The compliance score flatters UniiChat slightly: a check counts only when the turn changed files, so its five undone tasks appear as 31 ungradable checks, not failures.\n- The bulk answers were graded 'pass' in both arms with 8 extra sites listed in the first one (n=28 expected). The grader is lenient there, equally for both.\n- Summary quality (E4, verbatim-substring test, a lower bound): of 232 planted items, 154-159 survive at the leaf and 17-25 at level 5, at every effort.\n\nRound 1 for comparison: 61 of 61 recall in the UniiChat arm and all correct in the baseline, at 26-42 messages.",
  "compactor_tradeoff": "E4, one corpus of 128 messages, one run per candidate. All compaction figures below recomputed from the rows and match.\n\n| | Haiku low | Haiku high | Haiku xhigh |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cost per built line, CLI USD | 0.00135 | 0.00246 | 0.00657 |\n| Wall per built line | 6.5 s | 15.5 s | 47.7 s |\n| First try within 512 bytes | 94/171 (55%) | 144/178 (81%) | 174/179 (97%) |\n| Sends per line | 1.86 | 1.19 | 1.03 |\n| Thinking tokens per send | 462 | 2,954 | 11,572 |\n| Sends that called a tool | 4 of 318 | 0 of 212 | 0 of 184 |\n| Standing rules at level 5 (n=13) | 9 | 12 | 12 |\n| Corrected values at level 3 / level 5 (n=10) | 6 / 7 | 10 / 10 | 10 / 8 |\n| Sonnet reader, 40 questions | 38 | 40 | 38 |\n| Haiku reader, two runs | 32, 31 | 35, 35 | 29, 31 |\n\nWhat low effort gives up against xhigh:\n- **Size fit on the first try:** almost half the lines need a resend, and one line was kept at 519 bytes.\n- **Discipline about tools:** 4 wasted sends in E4. In E5, where the compactor could see the shell tools, 11.2% of sends (266 of 2,383) ended as a tool call and had to be redone; this stalled the run once, at turn 37.\n- **Standing rules and corrections deep in the tree:** 3 fewer rules at level 5 and 4 fewer corrected values at level 3.\n- **Nothing measurable downstream:** the reader scores are within one run's noise (the Haiku reader's two runs differ by up to 2 of 40 on the same tree).\n\nWhat it gains: 4.9x lower cost and 7.3x less wall time per line; the whole tree in 593 s against 5,558 s.\n\nOther candidates:\n- **Haiku high** is the safer middle: no tool calls, 12 of 13 rules and 10 of 10 corrections at level 5, 40 of 40, at 1.8x low's cost. It was not used in the long replay.\n- **Haiku medium** costs 18% more than low and lost the most rules at level 5 (8 of 13).\n- **Sonnet** is not a cheaper compactor: 0.0150-0.0155 USD per line, about 11x Haiku low, with 0 thinking tokens at both efforts. Tested on 32 messages only (45 lines each) and 10 questions.\n- **A local model:** not tested; none was loaded and loading one was not allowed.\n\nMethod correction that came out of E4: the CLI's cost figure accumulates within a process. Summing it per row overstates retry-heavy arms (0.412 naive against 0.231 corrected for Haiku low; I get the same). Round 1's E1 USD figures for arms with retries are overstated for that reason; nobody recomputed them.",
  "numbers_that_did_not_recompute": [
    "E5 'the CLI's own Haiku/Sonnet price ratio is 0.0627': not a price ratio. Fitting the CLI's cost figure to usage gives exact prices per million tokens of input / cache write / cache read / output: Haiku 0.10 / 0.20 / 0.01 / 0.50, Sonnet 2 / 4 / 0.10 / 10, Opus 4 / 8 / 0.20 / 20 (residuals below 1e-15). Haiku is 1:20 of Sonnet on input, write and output. 0.0627 is USD-per-unit blended over a read weight that does not match the CLI's.",
    "E5 'at the CLI's prices the arms are level; the conclusion depends on the Haiku price': the gap between the unit result (UniiChat 18% cheaper at turn 112) and the USD result (level) comes from the Sonnet cache-read price, 0.05x input in the CLI against 0.1x in the unit. Repricing all rows at the CLI's weights reproduces the USD picture (3.01M against 3.00M). With the unit as defined and Haiku at 0.05, UniiChat plus compactions is 3,597,811 and the cumulative crossover is turn 82, not 89.",
    "E5 checkpoint column 'A+comp': I get 482,909 / 1,420,160 / 3,261,190 / 3,772,166 at turns 25 / 50 / 100 / 112 against 479,210 / 1,419,704 / 3,259,896 / 3,771,656. Differences of 0.01-0.8% from how compaction sends are assigned to turns. Haiku sends at those turns: 474 / 1,125 / 2,140 / 2,383 against 460 / 1,124 / 2,137 / 2,383. Per-stretch compaction means differ the same way (5,841 / 9,608 / 6,456 / 8,619 / 8,139 against 5,697 / 9,744 / 6,465 / 8,585 / 8,209).",
    "E5 'about 2.5x cheaper per turn by turn 110': true of turn calls only (86,316 / 34,442). With compactions it is 2.0x.",
    "E5 '3 of the 7 fact messages reached': the turn rows hold 8 fact turns (12, 17, 21, 27, 47, 65, 89, 101).",
    "E5 '266 sends failed that way (Bash 165, zoom 102, date 4, Edit 1, Read 1)': 266 sends is right; the per-tool figures are tool calls (273), not sends. More important, 159 of the 165 shell calls and the 1 file read were executed, not refused. E5 does not say this.",
    "E5 'against 4 of 171 in E4': the E4 figure is 4 of 318 sends; 171 is the number of lines.",
    "E5 turn rows show 2,309 nodes at the last turn; the tree on disk has 2,318, as reported.",
    "E4 'most tries 6' for Haiku low: the highest send number in the rows is 5. Six may count a node that was rebuilt after a failed session; not reconciled.",
    "E4 Haiku reader, run 1, Haiku-low candidate: the stored answers field scores 17 of 20 because the model mislabelled two blocks; 32 of 40 comes only from re-parsing the reply text. I reproduced 32 with the harness's own parser.",
    "E6 'compactions cost 4,393,769 Haiku units over 49 sends': the 49 compaction rows in E3/sonnet-A sum to 4,344,208 at W=2 (1.1% lower). Output share 96% matches. Not reconciled; the pi-shape figure derived from it inherits the gap.",
    "E5 arm B2 is labelled 'stock session' but ran with empty setting sources and strict MCP: it is stock Claude Code tools and skills, not the owner's configuration. E6's 27,295 is the owner's configuration. The two figures (23,690 and 27,295) are different things and both are correctly measured.",
    "Matched to rounding: all E4 compaction rows for six arms (lines, sends, first and final fit, tokens, wall, tree build time, cache share, units and USD per line, tool calls, naive against corrected USD); E4 Sonnet-reader scores, block A scores, misses, zoom counts, requests and USD; E4 planted-item survival at every level and the priority-1 count; E5 turn-call units, USD, requests, view bytes, messages, prompt tokens at every checkpoint, all six crossover turns on the reported basis, batches, lookback rows, cache shares, wall and wait statistics, Opus and B2 totals, tree size and over-limit count; E6 block sizes, stock overhead, 5-minute turn costs and read shares, mid-change and expiry rows; round-1 E3 totals at both write prices; all ledger totals.",
    "Not recomputed, taken as reported: E5 recall, compliance and trap grades (read from analysis.json; grader not rerun); E4 order-key, standing-rule and correction counts (regex in the harness); E4 matched 0-31 figures for the Haiku arms; every pi paper number in E6; the 43 tests on commit 098433d."
  ],
  "isolation_and_credentials": "**Flags.** 3,926 call rows in round 2 (E4 1,210; E5 2,665; E6 51) plus one stock-call usage row.\n- Every row has `--setting-sources \"\"`, `--strict-mcp-config` and `--permission-mode dontAsk`.\n- `--disable-slash-commands` and `--no-session-persistence` are on every row except where the design needs otherwise: arm B, B2 and Opus-B resume a session; B2 also drops the slash-command and tool restrictions on purpose (21 tools, 17 skills loaded).\n- Init events on all rows: no hook events, API key source 'none', at most Claude Code's 2 built-in plugins, MCP servers either the trial's `chat` server or none.\n- One call was not isolated by design: E6's stock call ran once with the owner's normal configuration, so his plugins and hooks ran once.\n\n**Nothing carried between UniiChat turns: yes.** 112 turn rows, 112 distinct session ids, none shared with a compaction session, no `--resume`. The first request of a turn read as little as 4,227 tokens from cache, which is the tools-and-system prefix alone.\n\n**The baseline was one continued session: yes.** One session id on all 112 rows, `--resume` on 111 of them, cumulative cost rising monotonically to 5.995 USD, 0 compaction events. The Opus-B questions are 10 forks of that same session.\n\n**Finding not in the E5 report: compactions executed shell commands.** Arm A's Haiku compaction calls were given Read, Write, Edit and Bash with no allow list. Claude Code runs read-only shell commands without asking, so 159 shell commands and 1 file read executed in the arm's repository. All were no-ops or reads (echo, true, printf, and 4 that listed or printed repository files). Every write attempt was denied (6 shell commands including an append to a test file, 1 edit). So the repository was not changed by a compaction, on the evidence of the rows, but the compactor was not sandboxed from it.\n\n**Credentials and email (counts only).**\n- Owner's email address: 0 matches in results/E4, E5, E6, the ledger, chats and scratch.\n- Key-like strings (API-key prefixes, bearer tokens, JWT shapes, access or refresh token fields, ANTHROPIC_* assignments): 0 matches.\n- Generic email-shaped strings: 140, all synthetic test domains or image file names.\n- E6's captured request bodies: the email block is replaced and the authorization header is reduced to a class label and a length.\n- OMP credential: I read only the provider and type columns. Anthropic is one row of type oauth. No ANTHROPIC_* variable is set in this environment.\n\n**Caveats.**\n- E6 sent the real login token to a local 127.0.0.1 sink to capture Claude Code's request shape, as round 1 did. Nothing reached Anthropic from that.\n- Arm B's sessions are stored by Claude Code under the owner's config directory. I did not read there.\n- Three of my own command outputs were archived by the owner's output hook (obs-3864704253ee, obs-29cf2b9cc30b, obs-571b4b698583). They hold synthetic trial output and flags only.\n- No trial process is running. The core is at 098433d on trial/optchat-uniichat with a clean working tree; I changed nothing in the trial area.",
  "still_untested": [
    "The gist's central claim: better behaviour than a session that has auto-compacted or rotted. The baseline never compacted in 112 turns and made no errors; about 300+ turns would be needed to fill its 1,000,000-token window (inferred).",
    "Turns 113-260 of the E5 script. Everything past turn 112 is extrapolation, including whether UniiChat's lead keeps widening.",
    "Any request through pi. All pi numbers are paper: whether the per-request billing hash breaks the system-prompt cache, real usage figures, and whether a 5-minute mark is honoured on that route.",
    "The long replay with 5-minute cache writes. The W=1.25 columns are repriced from 1-hour runs and assume no pause over 5 minutes; a compaction-heavy run may well have such pauses.",
    "The true cache-read price. The CLI's estimate uses 0.05x input for Sonnet and Opus; the gist and the cost unit use 0.1x. I did not check either against a price list, and a subscription shows no price at all.",
    "A higher-effort compactor (Haiku high) in the long replay, and whether it removes the 11.2% tool-call failures and the tail waits.",
    "A separate prompt, or a tool-free request, for compactions. This would test whether the 12 turns answered as compaction lines and the compactor's shell calls go away, at the cost of the shared cache entry.",
    "A stock Claude Code baseline beyond 37 turns, and one running in a real project with the owner's configuration.",
    "Opus as the turn model over a long chat. The Opus phase was 10 late questions per arm from the Sonnet end state.",
    "Repeats. Every arm in both rounds is one run on one corpus or script, so no number has a variance estimate; the only repeat (E4's Haiku reader) moved by up to 2 of 40.",
    "A full Sonnet compaction tree (32 of 128 messages were built) and any non-Claude or local compactor.",
    "A human or model judge for grading; all grades are regex and file checks.",
    "Whether an agent breaks a standing order that has dropped out of every view line over a much longer horizon. Within 112 turns none was broken.",
    "Cache details: a spare mark surviving a batch, 16-line blocks, entry lifetimes between 20 s and 330 s, an account switch.",
    "Parts of the gist never built: handing a message to a running call, pages for long messages, subagents and zoom by name, images, per-turn device state. Also its 3,000-message cache replay and 30,000-message batching comparison."
  ],
  "questions_for_the_author": [
    "What baseline do the savings refer to: a continued session, a session that auto-compacts, or fresh sessions with a memory file? And on which model and context window?",
    "At what chat length were they measured? Here UniiChat is dearer below roughly 50-90 tool-heavy turns and only then pulls ahead.",
    "What do the compactions cost you, as a share of total spend, at Haiku xhigh? We measured about 11,500 thinking tokens and 46 s per 512-byte line at xhigh.",
    "What is the denominator of 98.6% and 96.2%: all input tokens, or only the tools-system-view prefix? Do they come from the cache model alone, or were they checked against real usage?",
    "Which cache-read and cache-write prices does the design assume, and does 'don't buy 1-hour entries' still hold for a chat a person types into with pauses over 5 minutes?",
    "Are compactions really sent the full tool list, shell included? If so, how often do they call a tool, and what stops a read-only shell command from running?",
    "How often does a turn answer as if it were a compaction (a summary line in place of doing the task)? We saw 12 of 112 turns on Sonnet with the verbatim prompt.",
    "What happens after a turn that adds more than about 84 lines (a 40-tool-call turn)? We measured a full view rewrite on the next call.",
    "Is there evidence that standing orders still govern behaviour once they are gone from every view line, and over how many messages?",
    "Why xhigh? Did lower efforts fail a downstream test, or only miss the 512-byte limit more often on the first try?",
    "How long do users wait in the tail, not on the median, with 8 compactions in flight after a large tool-heavy turn?",
    "Was the design run through a harness that adds its own text around the message (Claude Code, pi under a subscription login), or only on the raw API with a key?"
  ],
  "calls_used": "I made no model calls. Round-2 totals from /mnt/zer0models/z0-wt/salvage/optchat-trial/results/ledger-r2.jsonl (reservations minus settlements, recomputed): Haiku 3,529 of 4,000, Sonnet 887 of 900, Opus 40 of 80, pi route 0 of 400.\n\n| Experiment | Haiku | Sonnet | Opus |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| E4 | 1,099 | 235 | 0 |\n| E5 | 2,387 | 643 | 40 |\n| E6 | 43 | 9 | 0 |\n\nThese match the three reports. Every rate-limit event in the rows has status 'allowed'. The only errored rows are the 271 compaction sends that ended in a tool call (5 in E4, 266 in E5) and one E5 turn row.\n\nRound 1 for reference: Haiku 1,082, Sonnet 205, Opus 56.\n\nWhat I did: read-only recomputation with scripts run from stdin; nothing was written under the trial area or the repo. One read-only query of two non-secret columns (provider, credential type) in OMP's credential table to confirm E6's statement.\n\nFiles I relied on, all under /mnt/zer0models/z0-wt/salvage/optchat-trial/:\n- results/final-report.json (round 1)\n- results/E4/{haiku-low,haiku-medium,haiku-high,haiku-xhigh,sonnet-low,sonnet-medium}/calls.jsonl, results/E4/{qa,qa_haiku,qa_haiku2}/answers.jsonl, results/E4/corpus/\n- results/E5/{A,B,B2,opus-A,opus-B}/{calls,turns}.jsonl, results/E5/analysis.json\n- results/E6/{thin,turns5m,mid5m,gap5m}/index.jsonl, results/E6/stock/usage.jsonl, results/E6/capture/shapes.txt\n- results/E3/{sonnet,haiku,scaled}-A/calls.jsonl and {sonnet,haiku}-B/calls.jsonl\n- chats/e4-*/tree and chats/e5-A/tree\n- repo (branch trial/optchat-uniichat, head 098433d)"
}

