core_changes None by me: no commit, no edit under the trial repo.

- **5-minute arm:** done in the E6 harness only. `e6lib.set_ttl("5m")` swaps `claude_cli.blocks_of` in-process so the view mark carries no `ttl`, and the call passes `CLAUDE_CODE_PROMPT_CACHE_TTL=5m` in the child environment.
- **Suggested core change, not made:** `blocks_of` hard-codes `ttl="1h"` and `Session._content` calls it with the default. Make the lifetime a `ClaudeCLI` parameter, record it in each row, and pass the matching environment variable. Note that `child_env` strips `CLAUDE*` variables, so the variable has to go through the per-call `env`.
- **Repo head:** now 098433d, committed by another round-2 experiment. My runs imported the core at whatever head was checked out at the time; only `claude_cli.py`, `prompt.py` and `turn.tools_config` were used.
limits - **Every pi number is inferred.** It is measured Claude Code usage minus the two blocks, repriced. No request went through pi to Anthropic.
- **The paper model assumes** pi's tools-and-system segment costs the same 1,641 tokens (names and identity line differ by about 10 bytes), that Anthropic ignores the changing billing hash for caching, and that a 5-minute mark is honoured on pi's route as it was on Claude Code's.
- **Block sizes are n=1 each**, measured by adding the block's text to a message. The email was a placeholder of similar length, so the 170 could be off by a few tokens.
- **Stock overhead is n=1**, on Haiku, in an empty directory with no project files. A real project session will differ.
- **E3 was repriced, not rerun.** The 5-minute rows for E3 assume the same token counts as at 1 hour (true 32 of 32 times in E2) and no pause over 5 minutes between turns. Round 1's serial compaction waits were often longer than that, so a real 5-minute run of that harness would have paid cold rewrites.
- **The stripping rule for multi-request turns** (environment block written once per turn, read on later requests) is an assumption from the captured shape, not measured.
- **The continued-session baseline was left as measured.** It also carries the reminder block; on pi it would save roughly 1,400 units over 26 turns, which I ignored.
- **The cost unit weighs output at 5x and a 1-hour write at 2x.** On a subscription no price is observable, so these are list-price weights, not money.
- **5-minute expiry is one sample at 330 s.** Nothing between 20 s and 330 s was tested.
- **One run per arm, one corpus.** The output-token differences that make up the remaining gap come from single round-1 runs and carry no variance estimate.
- **Captured pi shapes are from SDK source 18.9.2**; the installed `omp` binary is 18.10.1 and may differ.
- **Not tested:** a spare mark surviving a batch, compactions on pi, 16-line blocks, any chat past 42 turns.
