**5. Compactions (Haiku xhigh), Sonnet chat, 49 sends:** cost 4,393,769 in Haiku token units, 96% of it output including thinking. The pi shape removes 527 input tokens per send, giving 4,317,923 (-1.7%, paper).
findings ['Measured, contradicts round 1: the 1-hour write at 2x is not forced by Claude Code. `CLAUDE_CODE_PROMPT_CACHE_TTL=5m` (also `FORCE_PROMPT_CACHING_5M` and a `promptCacheTtl` setting) makes all four marks 5-minute. On the subscription every written token then came back as a 5-minute write: 41,292 tokens over 24 Haiku turns and 14,460 over 8 Sonnet turns, with token counts identical to round 1.', 'The trial core itself hard-codes `ttl: "1h"` on the view mark (`claude_cli.py`, `blocks_of`). Round 1 listed 5-minute marks as not tested, yet concluded the lifetime could not be chosen.', "Measured: the owner's hypothesis is right for stock Claude Code and mostly irrelevant to round 1. A stock call with his normal configuration carries 27,295 input tokens before any content (n=1); round 1 ran a thinned Claude Code at 2,253. What Claude Code still adds there is two blocks, 527 tokens per call: a 170-token reminder before the view and a 357-token environment block after the message.", "Round 1 reported those two blocks as about 240 and 356 tokens; I measured 170 and 357 by difference (n=1 each). Round 1's stock figure was 28,793; mine is 27,295 (n=1, empty cwd).", "The environment block costs more than its size suggests. It sits after the message, so it is written again on every turn: 357 of about 1,720 written tokens per warm Haiku turn in E2, or 21% of each turn's cache write.", "Pi facts (no calls). `omp` is 18.10.1, a compiled binary at `~/.local/share/tern/omp/versions/18.10.1`. The pi-ai SDK source on disk is 18.9.2, at `/mnt/zer0models/home-offload/kvn/bun/install/global/node_modules/@oh-my-pi/pi-ai`. The captured shapes are from 18.9.2, not the binary's version.", "Credential type: OMP's Anthropic credential is a subscription OAuth login, not an API key. It is one row in `~/.omp/agent/agent.db`, table `auth_credentials`, `credential_type` oauth, with an OAuth-class access token plus refresh and expiry fields. No `ANTHROPIC_*` variable is set and there is no Anthropic API-key row. Per the SDK source, environment `ANTHROPIC_OAUTH_TOKEN` then `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` would be used if set (`registry/hooks/env.ts`), and the request shape is chosen by token form (`utils/anthropic-auth.ts`). No key material was printed or copied.", "Under that login pi-ai does not send [tools][system][view][message]. It sends the Claude Code billing header with a computed `cch` hash, the Claude Code identity line, tools renamed `_zoom` and `_date`, a `claude-cli/2.1.280` user agent and `claude-code-20250219` in the beta header. The source calls these values 'spoofed' and 'cloaking'. The gist's exact request comes out only with an API key.", 'The caller does control everything else in pi-ai: the system prompt (after the two forced blocks), the tool list, message blocks, all four cache marks and their lifetime through `onPayload` or `cacheRetention`, and effort through `reasoning` (thinking is disabled when it is not passed). Usage comes back as input, output, cache read and cache write, with a 5-minute/1-hour split in `usage.cttl`.', 'Pi\'s default lifetime on a subscription login is also 1 hour, by design (\'match Claude Code\'s native policy\'). Running on pi would not have changed the write price unless `cacheRetention: "short"` was passed.', 'Unmeasured risk on pi: the billing hash in system[0] changes on every request and sits inside the system-prompt cache prefix. If Anthropic does not strip that block before caching, the whole view would be rewritten on every call. Real Claude Code sends the same kind of header, so this probably works, but I did not test it.', "Decomposition of round 1's extra turn cost (repriced from round-1 rows; pi columns are paper). Sonnet, 26 turns, extra 33,963 units: dropping the two blocks removes 19,540, pricing writes at 1.25x removes 21,700, both together 34,151, which is all of it against the continued session as measured.", "Like for like (both arms at 1.25x) the Sonnet gap is 9,431 units, or 1.14x. Of that, 6,785 is the UniiChat arm's extra output (6,509 against 5,152 tokens); the input side is 6% above the continued session.", 'Haiku, 26 turns: the like-for-like gap is 23,311 units (1.33x). Extra output alone is 29,795 units (11,170 against 5,211 tokens), so the input side is 14% below the continued session. Scaled Haiku, 42 turns: gap 56,822 (1.53x), extra output 52,885, input side 5% above.', 'So once the harness blocks and the 2x price are removed, the input side is within about 15% of a continued session at 26-42 messages. What remains is output: fresh-view turns write longer replies and, in the scaled chat, make zoom calls (53 requests against 48).', "Compaction cost is untouched by the harness question. In the Sonnet chat it was 96% output tokens (Haiku xhigh thinking); the pi shape would trim 1.7%. Round 1's 5x and 58x with compactions stand on any harness.", "Measured, n=1: a 5-minute entry was gone after a 330-second pause and the whole view was written again. Inferred from the measured sizes: at an 80 KB view a cold rewrite costs about 41,900 units at 1.25x, while 1-hour entries cost about 1,000-1,300 units more per warm turn. One-hour entries are therefore cheaper once more than roughly 1 turn in 32-41 follows a 5-60 minute pause. For a chat a person types into, the gist's 'don't buy 1-hour entries' is likely the wrong advice.", "Mid-view change is the same on either harness with the gist's two marks: one changed line rewrote the whole 64 KB view (27,034 tokens), identical at 5 minutes and 1 hour. Pi's spare marks could in principle pin an older part of the view through a batch; I did not test that.", "Compactions sharing the turns' tools-and-system entry when both are Haiku: already true on Claude Code in round 1 (a cold compaction read the 1,641-token entry). Pi adds nothing here, and I made no pi measurement of it.", 'My own side effect, repaired: the pi-ai SDK wrote 11 request dumps to `~/.omp/logs/http-400-requests/` and 2 log files to `~/.omp/logs/` during the sink capture. They held only dummy-token requests to 127.0.0.1. I checked they were mine by PID and timestamp and deleted them; the directory, which predates this run, is left in place and empty.', "The stock call ran once with the owner's normal configuration, so his plugins and hooks ran once. It ended at `--max-turns 1` with a tool call pending (one request, 357 output tokens). I did not inspect what his hooks wrote.", 'The trial repo head is now 098433d (background compactor), committed by another round-2 experiment, not by me.']
decision_or_answer **Most of round 1's extra turn cost was the harness and its settings, not the design, and you do not need pi to remove most of it.**

- **How much was harness:** for Sonnet at 26 turns the 1.45x came from two things in about equal parts. Claude Code's two added blocks (527 tokens per call, 357 of them rewritten every turn) account for 19,540 of the 33,963 extra units. Pricing writes at 2x instead of 1.25x accounts for 21,700. Together they cover all of it.
- **The 2x price was a setting:** `CLAUDE_CODE_PROMPT_CACHE_TTL=5m` gives 5-minute writes on the subscription, measured this round. That alone takes Sonnet from 1.45x to 1.16x against the continued session as measured.
- **What is inherent at short lengths:** with both arms at the same price, UniiChat is still 1.14x (Sonnet), 1.33x (Haiku) and 1.53x (scaled, 42 turns). Nearly all of that is extra output tokens from fresh-view turns and zoom calls; the input side is within about 15% either way.
- **Compactions are unaffected:** they are 96% thinking output on Haiku xhigh, so the 5x-58x figure does not move on pi. Only a cheaper summarizer changes that.
- **Does pi change the conclusion:** on paper, pi with 5-minute marks reaches 1.00x of the continued session as measured for Sonnet, 1.17x for Haiku and 1.38x scaled. That is parity at best at these lengths, and still no saving. The crossover question still needs the long replay.
- **Stock Claude Code bloat is real but was not in round 1:** 27,295 tokens per call with your normal configuration against about 1,726 on pi, roughly 16x. Round 1 ran a thinned Claude Code at 2,253.

**Decision for you:** whether to run the live pi replay. The harness is not built; the capture script and cases are. It would send up to 400 requests on your subscription through a client that identifies itself to Anthropic as Claude Code with a computed attestation. It would confirm two things the paper cannot: that the per-request billing hash does not break the system-prompt cache, and the real usage numbers. I would not expect it to change the answer above.

Whatever you decide on pi, the cheap next step is to make the view mark's lifetime a parameter in the core and rerun with 5-minute marks.
