findings ["Measured: the cost crossover exists on turn calls. Arm A's turn cost flattens at about 27,000-34,000 units per turn once the view saw-tooths, while arm B's climbs with its prompt (13,699 to 86,316 per turn, prompt 4,852 to 344,607 tokens). Per turn, A is cheaper from about turn 48-49; cumulatively from turn 57 (W2) or 32 (W1.25). n = 112 turns, one run.", "Measured: with Haiku-low compactions counted at the CLI's own prices, the per-turn crossover moves to turn 57-58 and the cumulative one to turn 80-89 in units; in CLI USD the two arms are level at turn 112 (6.02 vs 6.00). Round 1's 5x-58x compaction penalty is gone at low effort: compaction is 23% of arm A's USD (1.37 of 6.02).", "Inferred, and it matters: the CLI prices Haiku at 0.0627 of Sonnet per unit. Compaction used 13.7M Haiku units (W2), 4.7 times arm A's turn units. At a one-third price ratio arm A would total about 7.5M units against B's 4.6M, with no crossover by turn 112.", "Measured, contradicts the gist's premise for this setup: the continued session never auto-compacted and never forgot. Sonnet has a 1,000,000-token window here; B answered 54 of 54 recall questions and broke no standing order. The gist's failure mode did not appear in 112 tool-heavy turns; what did appear is linear cost growth.", "Measured, new failure in arm A: the Sonnet turn sometimes answers the user's message as if it were a compaction, writing a summary line ('user (msg 323): ... Not started, no edits') instead of acting. This happened on 12 of 112 turns: 9 of the 13 order and correction messages, 3 of the 7 fact messages reached, and 1 task; four more tasks came back as status lines of the same kind. In all, 5 of 73 edit turns were not carried out (35, 49, 50, 59, 80); arm B had none.", "Measured consequence: the rounding correction at turn 50 was never applied in arm A's repository, and A later reported it as 'on hold' both on Sonnet (turn 99) and on Opus. That is the one substantive recall error; the other 3 misses are one turn (105) that spent all 8 requests on zoom calls and was cut off.", "Measured: where arm A did act, it obeyed every standing order and later correction, including orders 50-100 turns old sitting in level 5-10 lines: 351 of 351 gradable checks and 27 of 27 trap checks. Low-effort lines did not lose the orders within this horizon, against E4's warning.", 'Measured: zoom was hardly used. 12 calls on 4 of 112 turns, all on recall questions, none on a task or probe; the 512-byte lines were enough for the rest.', 'Measured: Haiku at low effort tries to call tools during compaction far more often in a tool-heavy chat than E4 saw. 266 of 2,383 sends (11.2%) failed that way (Bash 165, zoom 102, date 4, Edit 1, Read 1), against 4 of 171 in E4. Each failure is a wasted send and delays the line; this is what stalled turn 37. Sends per model-built node were 2.12; 11 of 2,318 nodes ended over 512 bytes.', 'Measured: the sawtooth behaves as the gist says. Three batches merged 263-348 pairs and took the view from 118-128 KB down to 64-67 KB; the largest view sent was 127,752 bytes. The turn after a batch rewrote the whole view: 30,254-34,647 tokens of cache write.', "Measured, confirms round 1's lookback finding: after each 44-file turn (90-95 new lines) the next turn rewrote the whole view (30,515 and 33,138 tokens written, only the 4,227-token prefix read).", "Measured cache-read share of all input tokens: arm A turns 95.6%, compactions 93.0%, arm B 98.9%. The gist's 98.6% and 96.2% were not reached, and the continued session caches better than UniiChat.", "Measured: 'the wait takes seconds' holds at low effort on the median (3.8 s) but not in the tail (p90 19.4 s, max 95 s after a 44-file turn). Turn calls themselves were slightly faster in arm A (11.4 s vs 13.1 s mean).", "Measured: Claude Code's stock overhead is about 18,800 tokens per request over the thinned session (23,690 vs 4,852 on the first request, 21 tools vs 4). Over 37 turns that made the stock session 42% dearer in units than the thinned one (889,826 vs 625,676) and 33% dearer than arm A's turn calls. This supports the owner's point that the harness adds a constant per-request load; a stock baseline would move the turn-call crossover earlier.", 'Measured, a design artefact rather than a UniiChat result: on Opus, arm B cost 25 times arm A (7.25M vs 0.29M units), because each forked question re-wrote the 347,000-token session to the Opus cache. A real model switch would pay that once, not ten times.', "Harness artefact to discount: my shell allow list refused compound commands with heredocs (7 of 458 tool calls in A, 5 of 244 in B). In 4 A turns and 1 B turn the agent then did not run `make verify` and said so; I graded those 'blocked', not failed.", "Arm A worked differently from arm B on the same tasks: 458 tool calls against 244 and 301 requests against 247. Under the gist prompt it leaned on shell commands; under Claude Code's prompt it used Read and Edit."]
decision_or_answer UniiChat through Claude Code breaks even with a continued session at roughly turn 50-60 on turn calls and turn 80-90 with compactions, and is clearly cheaper per turn after that (about 2.5x by turn 110). It does not yet buy better correctness: the continued session was perfect here, while UniiChat skipped 5 of 73 tasks.

What appeared from the gist's economics:
- Constant-size context: the view stayed within 64-128 KB over 1,161 messages.
- Flat per-turn cost against a linearly growing baseline.
- The batch sawtooth, with one full view rewrite per batch.
- Cheap compaction, once effort is low.

What did not appear:
- The baseline never forgot or auto-compacted, because the window is 1M tokens.
- "Nearly every token from cache" was weaker than the baseline (95.6% vs 98.9%).
- Zoom was almost unused.
- "The wait takes seconds" fails in the tail (up to 95 s).
- The single shared prompt confuses turns with compactions on 12 of 112 turns.

The conclusion on total cost depends on the Haiku price: at the CLI's ratio the arms are level at turn 112; at a one-third ratio UniiChat is still about 1.6x dearer.

Whether a leaner harness changes this is the pi arm's question. E5's evidence is that the stock Claude Code session carries about 18,800 extra tokens per request.
core_changes One commit on trial/optchat-uniichat, 098433d (on top of fd454e7), local only, no push. 43 tests pass (40 existing unchanged, 3 new).

- `compact.Background`: a thread in the chat's own process that reruns the compactor whenever a node is ready, so lines and merges are built while the turn call runs. `run_turn(background=...)` waits only for the lines before its message.
- `Compactor.poll`: a run used to block on its oldest in-flight call and miss messages appended meanwhile; with a background compactor it now looks at the queues every 0.05 s.
- `Chat.settle()`, called by `run_turn` after the wait and before render: runs a due batch on real line sizes. Without it, a turn of many long messages could leave the view far past 128,000 bytes and the next turn was refused. Which pairs merge is unchanged. In this run all three batches fired at an append, so this path was never the trigger.

Not fixed: the placeholder-size accounting inside `append` itself. Its effect was small here: batches ended at 65.6-67.5 KB, and 1 of 2,383 compaction contexts exceeded 32,000 bytes (32,362).

Harness choices outside the core:
- The arms' working directory is the scratch repository, which has no git metadata so the request prefix stays stable.
- The compactor's slack is 16,000 bytes.
- The user instructions ask for independent tool calls to be issued together.
- The commit reuses the branch's existing author identity through environment variables; no git config was changed.
