["Measured: the cost crossover exists on turn calls. Arm A's turn cost flattens at about 27,000-34,000 units per turn once the view saw-tooths, while arm B's climbs with its prompt (13,699 to 86,316 per turn, prompt 4,852 to 344,607 tokens). Per turn, A is cheaper from about turn 48-49; cumulatively from turn 57 (W2) or 32 (W1.25). n = 112 turns, one run.", "Measured: with Haiku-low compactions counted at the CLI's own prices, the per-turn crossover moves to turn 57-58 and the cumulative one to turn 80-89 in units; in CLI USD the two arms are level at turn 112 (6.02 vs 6.00). Round 1's 5x-58x compaction penalty is gone at low effort: compaction is 23% of arm A's USD (1.37 of 6.02).", "Inferred, and it matters: the CLI prices Haiku at 0.0627 of Sonnet per unit. Compaction used 13.7M Haiku units (W2), 4.7 times arm A's turn units. At a one-third price ratio arm A would total about 7.5M units against B's 4.6M, with no crossover by turn 112.", "Measured, contradicts the gist's premise for this setup: the continued session never auto-compacted and never forgot. Sonnet has a 1,000,000-token window here; B answered 54 of 54 recall questions and broke no standing order. The gist's failure mode did not appear in 112 tool-heavy turns; what did appear is linear cost growth.", "Measured, new failure in arm A: the Sonnet turn sometimes answers the user's message as if it were a compaction, writing a summary line ('user (msg 323): ... Not started, no edits') instead of acting. This happened on 12 of 112 turns: 9 of the 13 order and correction messages, 3 of the 7 fact messages reached, and 1 task; four more tasks came back as status lines of the same kind. In all, 5 of 73 edit turns were not carried out (35, 49, 50, 59, 80); arm B had none.", "Measured consequence: the rounding correction at turn 50 was never applied in arm A's repository, and A later reported it as 'on hold' both on Sonnet (turn 99) and on Opus. That is the one substantive recall error; the other 3 misses are one turn (105) that spent all 8 requests on zoom calls and was cut off.", "Measured: where arm A did act, it obeyed every standing order and later correction, including orders 50-100 turns old sitting in level 5-10 lines: 351 of 351 gradable checks and 27 of 27 trap checks. Low-effort lines did not lose the orders within this horizon, against E4's warning.", 'Measured: zoom was hardly used. 12 calls on 4 of 112 turns, all on recall questions, none on a task or probe; the 512-byte lines were enough for the rest.', 'Measured: Haiku at low effort tries to call tools during compaction far more often in a tool-heavy chat than E4 saw. 266 of 2,383 sends (11.2%) failed that way (Bash 165, zoom 102, date 4, Edit 1, Read 1), against 4 of 171 in E4. Each failure is a wasted send and delays the line; this is what stalled turn 37. Sends per model-built node were 2.12; 11 of 2,318 nodes ended over 512 bytes.', 'Measured: the sawtooth behaves as the gist says. Three batches merged 263-348 pairs and took the view from 118-128 KB down to 64-67 KB; the largest view sent was 127,752 bytes. The turn after a batch rewrote the whole view: 30,254-34,647 tokens of cache write.', "Measured, confirms round 1's lookback finding: after each 44-file turn (90-95 new lines) the next turn rewrote the whole view (30,515 and 33,138 tokens written, only the 4,227-token prefix read).", "Measured cache-read share of all input tokens: arm A turns 95.6%, compactions 93.0%, arm B 98.9%. The gist's 98.6% and 96.2% were not reached, and the continued session caches better than UniiChat.", "Measured: 'the wait takes seconds' holds at low effort on the median (3.8 s) but not in the tail (p90 19.4 s, max 95 s after a 44-file turn). Turn calls themselves were slightly faster in arm A (11.4 s vs 13.1 s mean).", "Measured: Claude Code's stock overhead is about 18,800 tokens per request over the thinned session (23,690 vs 4,852 on the first request, 21 tools vs 4). Over 37 turns that made the stock session 42% dearer in units than the thinned one (889,826 vs 625,676) and 33% dearer than arm A's turn calls. This supports the owner's point that the harness adds a constant per-request load; a stock baseline would move the turn-call crossover earlier.", 'Measured, a design artefact rather than a UniiChat result: on Opus, arm B cost 25 times arm A (7.25M vs 0.29M units), because each forked question re-wrote the 347,000-token session to the Opus cache. A real model switch would pay that once, not ten times.', "Harness artefact to discount: my shell allow list refused compound commands with heredocs (7 of 458 tool calls in A, 5 of 244 in B). In 4 A turns and 1 B turn the agent then did not run `make verify` and said so; I graded those 'blocked', not failed.", "Arm A worked differently from arm B on the same tasks: 458 tool calls against 244 and 301 requests against 247. Under the gist prompt it leaned on shell commands; under Claude Code's prompt it used Read and Edit."]
