4: study: optchat-claude-code-v0
6: author: "zer0 research"
7: date: "october 12, 2026"
13:     label: what we got wrong in round 1
42: The specification gives a view that grows from 64 to 128 KB before a batch of merges reduces it again. It describes cache writes priced at 1.25 times input and reads at about 0.1 times input. It also reports simulated cache and batching savings. It does not promise that fresh UniiChat calls beat a cached continued session at short lengths. We introduced that baseline to measure the cost of the implementation, so an expensive short chat was never a direct refutation of the gist.
46: Round 1 tested line writing, cache behaviour and short paired chats. Round 2 tested compactor effort, a longer engineering replay, and request overhead. The replay began with a synthetic repository and scripted editing tasks, corrections, facts, recall questions and tool-heavy reads. Both main arms received the same script. UniiChat started a fresh call per user message with Sonnet turns and Haiku-low compactions. The baseline continued one thinned Claude Code session on Sonnet with auto-compaction enabled as normal.
48: The experiment name referred to a 250-turn replay, but the generated script contained 260 turns. Both main arms completed 112. The run stopped at its reserved-call budget, rather than at a provider limit or a baseline failure. The following table counts provider requests from the ledgers, including auxiliary readers and probes. A turn can make several requests; a compactor retry is another send. These are measured programme counts, not independent samples.
52: | round 1, experiments and probes | 1,082 | 205 | 56 | short chats, cache tests and compaction tests |
53: | round 2, E4 | 1,099 | 235 | 0 | compactor sweep and readers |
54: | round 2, E5 | 2,387 | 643 | 40 | longer paired replay and auxiliary arms |
55: | round 2, E6 | 43 | 9 | 0 | overhead and cache-lifetime measurements |
57: The round-2 trial branch ended at `098433d`, a local commit adding background compaction and a pre-render settle step. Round 1 used `fd454e7`. The branch had not been pushed when these results were frozen. The study manifest pins the full revisions. No live pi request was made, and no local compactor was tested.
59: ## what we got wrong in round 1 {#corrections}
61: We said Claude Code's one-hour cache marks could not be changed. That was wrong. E6 set `CLAUDE_CODE_PROMPT_CACHE_TTL=5m` and measured five-minute writes on the subscription route. The trial core had also hard-coded one hour on its own view mark. Our earlier account acknowledged that we had not tested a five-minute mark, then treated the untested setting as a constraint of Claude Code.
63: The confirmation used synthetic warm sequences, with 24 Haiku turns and 8 Sonnet turns. Token counts matched the corresponding round-1 sequences exactly; only the write class changed. That establishes that the setting works in those samples. It does not establish that five minutes is cheapest for a human-paced conversation. In one expiry sample, a five-minute entry disappeared after a 330-second pause. Cold rewrites can outweigh cheaper writes when the user pauses.
65: We also found a cost-sum error in the round-1 E1 analysis script. Claude Code reports cumulative USD within a process. The script summed that cumulative value on every retry row, counting earlier spend again. In the new low-effort sweep, the same naive calculation gave USD 0.412 against USD 0.231 after taking differences, over 318 sends. The round-1 retry-heavy E1 USD figures have not been repaired individually. We retain them as historical reported figures with that defect recorded, rather than treating them as audited costs.
73: The longer replay crossed the real view threshold. It logged 1,161 messages and finished with 2,318 tree nodes. It produced three batches, at turns 52, 77 and 103, in one run. The largest view sent was 127,752 bytes. The batch result was followed by new messages, so end-of-turn sizes were above the low-water target; those sizes do not mean every batch stopped at that target.
77: | 52 | 348 | 124,266 | 65,652 |
78: | 77 | 263 | 118,428 | 67,467 |
79: | 103 | 276 | 127,752 | 65,634 |
81: The cost comparison below uses weighted tokens. One unit is an uncached input token of the turn model. The formula is input + W × cache write + 0.1 × cache read + 5 × output. Thinking is part of output, not an additional charge. W is 2 for the observed one-hour writes and 1.25 for five-minute repricing. These are accounting assumptions applied to measured usage, not money paid on the subscription.
83: To combine Sonnet turns with Haiku compactions, this table weights Haiku units at 0.05 of Sonnet units. That is the relative input, write and output price reconstructed from the CLI estimates. It keeps the gist-style read weight within each model's unit. The original E5 report used a blended coefficient as if it were a model price ratio. The final audit corrected that interpretation.
85: The checkpoint totals include all compaction sends assigned before the next turn-call starts, with the remaining sends assigned to the final turn. That matters because compaction runs in the background. The final audit found small discrepancies in E5's checkpoint assignments; the numbers here use its corrected assignment and the corrected model weight.
