150: Low effort cost 4.9 times less per built line than xhigh and matched its downstream score. It gave up first-try fit and kept fewer standing rules in the deepest tested lines. The one retained over-limit line at low effort was 519 bytes. More retries were still cheaper than xhigh's thinking. The original report's maximum-tries claim was not reconciled by the final audit, so it is not used here.
154: The Sonnet-reader scores are too close to rank the efforts confidently. Every effort had one tree and one reader run. A supplementary Haiku reader was repeated and moved by up to two correct answers out of 40 on the same tree. The observed tie between low and xhigh supports choosing a cheaper candidate for this trial; it does not demonstrate equal reliability on arbitrary chats.
156: Sonnet compaction was tested only on the first 32 messages, with 45 model-built lines per effort and 10 downstream questions per candidate. Both tested efforts answered all those questions, but their per-line CLI estimates were USD 0.0150-0.0155. The downstream view combined their partial trees with the rest of the Haiku-xhigh tree. That partial test gives no basis for calling Sonnet a cheaper compactor or comparing full-tree recall.
158: In the longer replay, low-effort compaction accounted for about 23% of the UniiChat arm's estimated USD, n=2,383 sends. The earlier roughly 5-times to 58-times whole-arm penalties have disappeared in this setup. This is a changed workload and setting, not a repaired estimate of what those earlier xhigh chats cost.
162: The baseline never encountered the failure that motivated the design. Claude Code reported a 1,000,000-token Sonnet window. Its continued session ended at 344,607 prompt tokens with no auto-compaction events over 112 turns. We observed neither recall loss nor broken standing orders in the graded checks. The gist's central comparison against a forgetful or compacted session remains untested here.
