181: A late Opus phase used the Sonnet endpoint, with 10 turns per arm. It scored 11 of 12 recall answers for UniiChat and 12 of 12 for the baseline. This was a late probe, not a long Opus replay. Its session-fork costs cannot establish Opus economics over a continuing conversation, and we do not use them for that claim.
183: The summary tests also distinguish disappearance from a coarse line from disappearance from storage. E4's exact-substring test found only 17-25 of 232 planted items at level 5, depending on effort. Paraphrases count as absent in that test. The reader could often recover details by zooming, so substring loss is not the recall error rate. Conversely, an agent must decide to zoom. Keeping the original log does not by itself prove that an omitted standing rule will govern future work.
187: The shared system prompt asks the same model to handle turns and compactions. In the replay, Sonnet sometimes summarized the new user message instead of doing the task. The harness identified 12 such turns out of 112. The final reviewer found fewer with a narrower regex, so the broader count should be read as a harness classification supported by inspected examples, not a uniquely defined detection rate.
