209: Waiting improved with low effort and background work. Across 112 turns, the estimated wait if the next message arrived immediately had a 3.8-second median and a 95.0-second maximum. The arms ran in lockstep on one account, so the waits actually paid differed from that estimate. These are measured scheduling observations, not isolated latency benchmarks for a person using the system.
211: The cache had a tool-heavy failure case too. Two turns added 95 and 90 lines. The next calls wrote the whole view again, consistent with exhausting the marked-prefix lookback. The second event overlapped a batch, so it is not an isolated causal test. A cheaper fresh-call design still pays for long bursts that invalidate its cached prefix.
215: Stock Claude Code carries substantial fixed request content. Round 1 already disabled user configuration and restricted tools, so its cost result cannot be explained by attributing stock overhead to it. E6 measured the smaller remaining blocks separately.
