227: The stock comparison ended at 37 turns, one run per arm. Stock tools and skills cost 889,826 units against 625,676 for the thinned session. That is a real overhead difference, but most of the fixed block is cached after its first use. It is not a fresh full-price charge on every turn, and the stock arm did not include the owner's normal configuration.
229: Pi was analysed on paper only. Captured request shapes and local SDK source let E6 estimate what removing Claude Code's two small wrapper blocks would save. They do not provide live pi usage, cache hits, correctness or elapsed time. Under a subscription login the captured pi shape still includes client-added system content. Whether that content preserves cache behaviour on the real route was not measured.
239: The observed endpoint is 112 of 260 scripted turns. Every claim about later cost, memory or compliance is inference. The baseline window remained comfortably below its reported capacity. The trial therefore measured growing cost against a still-successful session; it did not measure recovery from auto-compaction or worsening behaviour caused by context length.

